    affiliation: Posner Lab, Northern Arizona University
repository-code: https://github.com/elyfmiller/flubnf
license: MIT
keywords:
  - influenza
  - forecasting
  - FluSight
  - particle filter
  - compartmental model
  - rule-based modeling
  - BNGL
  - PyBNF
abstract: >-
  FluBNF forecasts weekly influenza hospital admissions for the 52 US
  jurisdictions of the CDC FluSight challenge. It submits two standalone
  models, each under its own hub identity, and blends nothing. Oracle SIHRS
  is mechanistic: an SIHRS compartmental model written in BNGL, fitted every
  week by a sequential particle filter in a fork of PyBNF on the bngsim
  engine, whose forecast growth is then combined (geometric mean, half and
  half) with donor growth from earlier seasons at the same calendar week.
  Groundhog is empirical: the last observed count scaled by the empirical
  quantiles of growth ratios seen at the same epiweek in strictly earlier
  seasons, pooled across jurisdictions, with a committed FluSurv-NET donor
  bank spliced in; nothing is fitted. In vintage-true retrospective replays
  of the 2023-24, 2024-25 and 2025-26 seasons, scored as weighted interval
  score relative to the CDC FluSight-baseline (ratio of WIS sums on shared
  cells), the Groundhog pools at 0.666 and the Oracle SIHRS at 0.738 against
  the plain filter's 0.819. All scores are self-computed from the hub's
  archived vintage data and baseline files, and are retrospective replays
  rather than real-time submissions. The 1.0 and 1.1 releases shipped a
  different product, an equal-weight ensemble; their record is in
  docs/archive/RELEASE-1.0.md.
